ho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161824/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161824"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161850/selection/285558a3f4acbf3b464d9184ce6a1f9d63dc45f5c542e6df1f40a50aafc1b4af" :answer/id #uuid "9bdfde05-9f5e-51f8-abe9-79f23af42287" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161850/selection/df5fb420f76d463889c03747e00a3bdb1557aa87d6b668f82a90db663ecde07a" :answer/id #uuid "0c583e81-9a38-55a5-977d-ff76021e6763" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161850/selection/24bec53a6b9c2b5c2797a260523b736b0ce42df8c2bdf88f13dc5e266a824375" :answer/id #uuid "6d77e5f7-be39-5f1a-abbf-feb1a215615b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161850/selection/1660449cff8539a69603ae0d65a90334d88b26c2e47c9ab630a07e93a2ea9523" :answer/id #uuid "2346c329-f2d8-5e96-9275-629533f90a44" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161850/selection/120826cd420a5eac571463d42f9222aa3cc6f742396b453a01cd8f9f67ed20a1" :answer/id #uuid "2d15d3a4-00d0-56e9-b659-9dc4de5f6b2c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161850/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2c91a51d-bb41-5a53-a44a-271ae7ad9f96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161850/selection/24bec53a6b9c2b5c2797a260523b736b0ce42df8c2bdf88f13dc5e266a824375" :answer-field/choices ["q-161850/selection/285558a3f4acbf3b464d9184ce6a1f9d63dc45f5c542e6df1f40a50aafc1b4af" "q-161850/selection/df5fb420f76d463889c03747e00a3bdb1557aa87d6b668f82a90db663ecde07a" "q-161850/selection/24bec53a6b9c2b5c2797a260523b736b0ce42df8c2bdf88f13dc5e266a824375" "q-161850/selection/1660449cff8539a69603ae0d65a90334d88b26c2e47c9ab630a07e93a2ea9523" "q-161850/selection/120826cd420a5eac571463d42f9222aa3cc6f742396b453a01cd8f9f67ed20a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161850" :question/id #uuid "a7566667-f808-5eb0-a535-838d177964ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-161850" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/dcd901b032075a4c4a22c991d9bb3e5e5ff49e7a2c91e7e840ede1516085f683.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2y,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/736c46d0121c646def56c6b8fede81a4155fffb97032facd75a6cdf29f98f65a.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161850/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161850"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161882/selection/54c544cfed27a39bd6139284df57d6823492b70e4f6ab4d73d22699961d4a69d" :answer/id #uuid "14693549-eed6-5820-9834-0840318668ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$