" "q-161882/selection/75e7e40a356c2cca139c0c18c33a148775a2e495821d6d78f0fec79317ef544b" "q-161882/selection/0104cee0649572727449bff037c3f0c1de7271ce28ea6a7d596256b63e99cf84"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161882" :question/id #uuid "084fb25a-6bf4-5dd3-8597-2be6a35f1998" :question/math-academy-id "q-161882" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/cc5c179ec93879776b7f033f8a62674fcf272ca0b1270cef0902905192d613f4.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the half-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+5z=0$ for $y\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $\\frac{5}{2}$ centered at $(0,0,-\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+5z=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+5z & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+5\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/22924aa3079443eb0c712afbb80bbe1b58407cec297a8ef4c90fb0e69aec9735.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-161882/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161882"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161854/selection/f2a29b130b572faa87d3acaca7324406e759f93aec7c36f6fdf77fc0406ac9c3" :answer/id #uuid "af41d341-bb9b-5abe-9143-299d5759a444" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161854/selection/f7f06e818871916f248c22d839d3392451648eecabddd7286398d3e584c15213" :answer/id #uuid "58012ece-490c-570b-8dab-086e2a1b31d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161854/selection/0713dedc7c0fdd4342133f38587f5f7414c12047257085605dced367b43ba166" :answer/id #uuid "727cef38-01da-50c0-b8d1-221f038465b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 16\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161854/selection/cb0e3ec840c00f3fb61e3dfe3458d2d449b578141586ecba30a9c8399e632209" :answer/id #uuid "83ac28f8-af55-5fc1-b52d-f6810724d9